// Car avoiding what costs money, on top of car.json: the vignette motorways of
// this region and the tolled passes, tunnels and bridges a vignette does not
// cover. `toll` is ALL where anyone pays and HGV where only lorries do, so a
// car reads the former alone.
//
// Blocked, not penalised. A priority multiplies an edge's travel time, so it
// can only ever express a cost per kilometre, and neither toll here is one: a
// vignette is a flat fee and a bridge toll is per crossing. Any finite value
// therefore buys detours proportional to the length of the tolled link rather
// than to what it costs — 0.02, against car.json's `distance_influence: 90`,
// was worth roughly ten times the distance. "Avoid tolls" is taken to mean
// avoid, so a destination reachable only across a tolled bridge returns no
// route. That is at least an honest answer.
//
// Where a way carries no `toll` tag GraphHopper fills the value in from the
// country (OSMTollParser.getCountryDefault). Slovak motorways and the R
// expressways come out ALL however OSM tags them, and the toll-free D1/D2
// sections keep the `toll=no` the tag wins with. German motorways come out
// HGV, so a `road_class == MOTORWAY` clause would have been wrong here and
// `toll` alone is right.
//
// Austria is the hole in that table: it is absent, so its ways default to NO,
// its vignette is not tagged `toll=*` in OSM, and the extract covers the
// country — hence the second clause. Autobahnen and Schnellstraßen are both
// `highway=motorway` there, which is the vignette's scope; `highway=trunk` is
// not, so it stays out. The Austrian tunnel tolls (Brenner, Tauern, Arlberg,
// Karawanken) are tagged and the first clause already has them.
{
  "priority": [
    { "if": "toll == ALL", "multiply_by": "0" },
    {
      "if": "country == AUT && road_class == MOTORWAY",
      "multiply_by": "0"
    }
  ]
}
